# Credentials for `go run ./tools/credential-matrix`.
#
# Copy to .env in this directory (gitignored) and fill in. Point this at a
# THROWAWAY organization: --destructive creates and deletes real records, and
# even a default run mints a short-lived project API key.
#
# The tool reports what each credential actually is before it starts probing —
# USER-bound, PROJECT-bound, or rejected outright — so you can confirm you
# supplied the class you meant rather than discovering it from the results.

# Organization API key.
# Dashboard -> organization settings -> API keys.
HOOKDECK_ORG_API_KEY=

# A USER-bound CLI session key: one that belongs to a person and can move
# between organizations, which is what `project use <org> <project>` needs.
#
# Get it by signing in through the browser:
#
#   hookdeck login
#
# then copy the api_key value out of ~/.config/hookdeck/config.toml into the
# line below. Open the file and copy it — do not cat or grep it, which would
# put a reusable credential into your terminal scrollback and any shell log.
#
# `hookdeck ci --api-key <project-api-key>` is the other way to get a CLI
# credential, and it is NOT what belongs here: it exchanges a Project API key
# for a credential pinned to that one project, which cannot move between
# organizations. (`--api-key` on `hookdeck login` is a hidden legacy root flag,
# not login's own; `ci` is the documented exchange.) The tool prints which
# class it got, so check the line says USER-bound.
HOOKDECK_CLI_SESSION_KEY=

# Optional. Left blank, a project key is minted with the organization key and
# deleted again at the end of the run.
HOOKDECK_PROJECT_API_KEY=
